---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import P from "../components/P.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import { dead } from "../lib/people.js";
import places from "../data/places.json";
import graves from "../data/australia-graves.json";
const here = dead.filter((p) => [p.birthPlace, p.deathPlace, p.burialPlace]
  .some((x) => (x || "").startsWith("Briatico") || (x || "").includes("(Briatico)")));
const pl = places.find((x) => x.name.startsWith("Briatico")) || {};
const surnames = [...new Set(here.map((p) => p.surname).filter(Boolean))].sort();
---
<Base title="Briatico" eyebrow="Vibo Valentia, Calabria"
      dek="The deepest line in this archive, and the one that does not carry the surname. A small comune on the Tyrrhenian coast of Calabria that sent its people to three different continents.">

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">14 September 2026 · the keystone</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Act 1 of 1849, and the family turns out not to be from Briatico</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>On <strong>14 June 1849</strong>, before Antonino Bajoni, sindaco of Briatico,
      <strong>Domenico Prostamo</strong> married <strong>Giovanna Ventrice</strong>. The act runs to
      nine lines and every one of them was new.</p>
      <p>He is <em>di anni trentasei</em> — thirty-six, so born about <strong>1813</strong>, where the
      family tree says 1820. He is a <strong>marinaro</strong>. And he is <em>nato in
      <strong>Pizzo</strong></em>: not Briatico, not Potenzoni, but a town eleven kilometres up the
      coast. He is <em>figlio di <strong>ANTONINO</strong>, di professione marinaro, domiciliato
      DEFUNTO, e di <strong>MARIA FRAGALÀ</strong>, domiciliata al Pizzo</em>.</p>
      <p>That single clause does two things. It <strong>documents the fourth generation</strong>,
      which this archive has carried as <em>untested</em> — family tree only, no source — since it
      began; and the tree's name for him, Antonino, turns out to be exactly right. It also produces
      <strong>Maria Fragalà</strong>, who was not in the tree at all: a widow still living at Pizzo
      while her son had moved along the coast.</p>
      <p>The bride is twenty-one and born at Briatico, <em>figlia di <strong>GIUSEPPE</strong>, di
      professione bracciale, domiciliato defunto, e <strong>CATERINA CICHELLO</strong></em>, who came
      to the wedding and gave her consent in person. Two more names, both new.</p>
      <p>And it explains the silence. Three complete years of Briatico births — 1818, 1819, 1820 —
      hold no Prostamo of any given name. Thirteen years of Briatico marriages hold none either. The
      reason is not that the register is thin. <strong>The Prostamo were not there yet.</strong>
      This act is the family's first appearance in the town, and the work now moves to
      <strong>Pizzo</strong>, whose registers this archive has never opened.</p>
      <p class="dek">One more line is worth keeping. The groom produced <em>la fede di morte del
      padre, e dell'<strong>avo paterno</strong></em> — his grandfather's death certificate. So the
      third generation was dead by 1849 and his death act exists. The tree, independently, puts
      Pasquale Prostamo's death at <strong>Pizzo</strong> on 4 January 1829. Two things agreeing is
      not a source, and the generation stays marked untested until that act is opened — but they
      agree, and they agree about Pizzo.</p>
    </div>
    <figure style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <img src={u("/plates/briatico-matr1849-atto1.jpg")} alt="Briatico, Matrimoni 1849, atto 1"
           style="width:100%;height:auto;border:1px solid var(--rule)" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption class="dek" style="margin-top:.6rem">Briatico, Stato civile della restaurazione,
      Matrimoni 1849, atto 1 — Archivio di Stato di Catanzaro (Antenati an_ua87663, image 2).</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">14 September 2026 · the marriage bundle</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Antonino Prostamo died in the cholera, and the bundle proves the line</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>Every Italian marriage leaves a second file — the <em>processetti</em>, the loose papers the
      couple had to produce. Briatico's for 1849 survive, and the first bundle in the volume is
      <P n="Domenico Prostamo" />'s. It settles four things at once.</p>
      <p><strong>His father's death.</strong> On 20 May 1849 the archivist of Pizzo certified that,
      having searched the death register, <em>«ha ritrovato in quello <strong>DI MORTI COLERICI
      dell'anno 1837</strong> la seguente partitola»</em> — and copied out act <strong>327</strong>:
      <em>«A dì 31 Dicembre anno milleottocento trentasette ad ore due di notte è morto ANTONINO
      PROSTAMO di anni 56, marinaro, domiciliato alla Ripa della Marinella, marito di MARIA FRAGALÀ
      del Pizzo.»</em></p>
      <p>He died <strong>in the cholera</strong>, at two in the morning on the last night of 1837.
      Pizzo kept its cholera dead in a <strong>book of their own</strong>, apart from the ordinary
      <em>Morti</em> series — which is why he could have been hunted through those volumes for a very
      long time without being found. Fifty-six puts his birth about <strong>1781</strong>, and that
      agrees with the thirty-two he gave when he declared his son's birth in 1814. Maria Fragalà
      outlived him by at least twelve years.</p>
      <p><strong>The descent.</strong> Filed beside it is a certified extract of <em>Pizzo, Morti
      1829, act 7</em> — Pasquale Prostamo's death — produced as the groom's <em>fede di morte
      dell'<strong>avo paterno</strong></em>. So Pasquale was Domenico's paternal grandfather, which
      is to say <strong>Antonino's father</strong>. The link this page called untested that morning is
      a document by the afternoon.</p>
      <p><strong>And the groom had another name.</strong> The bundle holds his birth extract: act
      <strong>285</strong>, born at eighteen o'clock on <strong>2 April 1814</strong> at Pizzo,
      <em>alle Moli</em>, to Antonino, 32, and Maria Fragalà, 27 — and entered in the register as
      <strong>ANTONIO</strong> Prostamo. So on the morning of the wedding he and four witnesses swore
      an <em>atto di notorietà</em> before the sindaco: the man registered in 1814 as Antonio
      <em>«chiamasi effettivamente DOMENICO PROSTAMO, e questo è stato sempre il suo effettivo
      nome»</em>. There he is <em>figlio del fu Antonino, di anni <strong>trentacinque</strong></em> —
      thirty-five, where the marriage act the same day says thirty-six.</p>
      <p><strong>The bride's side goes deeper still.</strong> Two Ventrice death extracts are in the
      bundle, exactly as the marriage act promised: her father <strong>Giuseppe Ventrice</strong>,
      dead at Briatico on 21 June 1839 at thirty; and her grandfather <strong>Domenico Ventrice</strong>,
      dead at <strong>Zambrone</strong> on 13 January 1815, a <em>bracciale</em>, husband of
      <strong>Anna Fama</strong> and son of the late <strong>Paolo Ventrice</strong> and the late
      <strong>Anna Pappa</strong>. Five names on the bride's side, none of them in the family tree.</p>
      <p><strong>Zambrone was opened for the original, and it is not there either.</strong> The comune
      has one pre-unification death volume on Antenati, <em>Morti 1811–1839</em>. It holds 1811, then
      1812 twice — once for Zambrone and once for <strong>San Giovanni</strong>, a hamlet that kept a
      register of its own — and then <strong>jumps straight to 1822</strong>. Every year from there is
      in order, 1822 through 1839. <strong>The years 1813 to 1821 are simply not in it</strong>, and
      the portal's Zambrone holdings then stop until 1911. So for the second time in a day, the
      clerk's copy in the marriage bundle is the only readable text of an act.</p>
      <p><strong>And the bride herself now has a day.</strong> Briatico's births for 1828 hold her at
      act 12: on <strong>23 June 1828</strong>, at nine in the morning, <strong>Giuseppe Ventrice</strong>,
      <em>di anni <strong>trentatré</strong></em>, a <em>bracciale</em>, came to the town hall and
      presented a girl born that morning <em>nella casa di loro propria abitazione</em> to
      <strong>Caterina Cichello</strong>, <em>di anni <strong>ventinove</strong></em>, and named her
      <strong>GIOVANNA</strong>. The parroco of <strong>San Nicola</strong> returned the baptism
      notice the next day.</p>
      <p>That one line moves her father fourteen years. His death extract of 1839 makes him
      <em>di anni trenta</em> — born about 1809. Here he stands in front of the sindaco and gives his
      own age as <strong>thirty-three</strong>, so born about <strong>1795</strong>, and about
      forty-four when he died. He cannot be both, and a man's own declaration outranks two neighbours'
      estimate at his funeral: this archive carries 1795 and keeps the conflict on the record. Caterina
      Cichello was born about 1799. And Giovanna was <strong>twenty</strong> at her wedding on 14 June
      1849 — nine days short of the twenty-one her marriage act claims.</p>

      <p><strong>Zambrone's marriages were opened next, and they cannot hold the couple either.</strong>
      Domenico was about fifty-eight when he died in January 1815 and Anna Fama about fifty; their son
      Giuseppe was born about 1809. Civil registration began in Calabria in <strong>1809</strong> and
      Zambrone's marriage volume begins in <strong>1811</strong>. A couple whose son was born in 1809
      married before the register existed — so their marriage is in the <strong>parish</strong> books,
      which Antenati does not hold. It is the same wall this archive met at Pizzo with Pasquale
      Prostamo, and it is where the Calabrian line stops being testable online.</p>
      <p>The volume is still worth reading, because every act and every annual index names the parents
      of both spouses — so the couple's <em>other</em> children would name them. Two years were read
      in full. <strong>1822</strong> holds six marriages and no Ventrice at all. <strong>1826</strong>
      holds six, and one Ventrice: <strong>Serafina Ventrice</strong> married Vincenzo Sorrentino on
      9 August 1826 — and she is <em>figlia di <strong>Francesco</strong> e di <strong>Caterina la
      Vessa</strong></em>. A second Ventrice household in the same small comune, and not ours. The
      remaining forty-odd indexes are mapped and unread.</p>

      <p class="dek">And the copy is not clean. It crams two ages into one clause — <em>sposo di Anna
      Fama di anni cinquanta … di anni cinquantotto, di professione bracciale</em>. The fifty-eight
      stands beside his trade and is read here as <strong>his</strong>, which puts his birth about
      1757; the fifty is read as his wife's. This page first said he was fifty. Where the original is
      gone, the ambiguity cannot be resolved — only recorded.</p>
      <p><strong>The register itself was looked for, and it is not there.</strong> Pizzo's deaths for
      1836–1840 are on Antenati in one volume of 427 images. Its ordinary register for 1837 runs to
      act 207 and then <strong>stops, on 16 July 1837</strong>. From act 209 the town was writing its
      dead into a <strong>second book</strong> — and that book has not been digitised. What has
      survived into the volume is its <strong>index</strong>, bound in after the ordinary register:
      twelve openings listing acts <strong>209 to 325</strong> with names, ages, trades and quarters.
      Antonino Prostamo is act <strong>327</strong>, two past the last line of it.</p>
      <p>So the extract above is not a convenience copy of something better. As far as this archive
      can reach, <strong>it is the only readable text of act 327</strong> — and it survives only
      because a clerk at Pizzo copied it out in May 1849 so that a sailor could marry at Briatico
      three weeks later.</p>
      <p class="dek">The shape of the volume is its own evidence. A town's ordinary death register
      breaking off in the middle of July, and a second one opened behind it under a certificate dated
      at Catanzaro that August, is what an epidemic looks like from inside an archive. The surviving
      index holds a hundred and seventeen of the dead between July and the end of the year; the
      register it indexes would hold at least two hundred more. Searched for Prostamo, the index has
      none — it stops two short of him.</p>

      <p class="dek">One correction, and it is mine. The chancery's copy of Pasquale's act makes him
      <em>sessantacinque</em> where the original register says <em>settantacinque</em> — ten years
      lost between an act and its own certified copy twenty years later. This archive carries the
      original's figure and records the copy's.</p>
    </div>
    <figure style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <img src={u("/plates/briatico-nati1828-atto12.jpg")} alt="Briatico, Nati 1828, atto 12"
           style="width:100%;height:auto;border:1px solid var(--rule)" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption class="dek" style="margin-top:.6rem">Briatico, Nati 1828, atto 12 — the birth of
      Giovanna Ventrice (Antenati an_ua87730, image 13).</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure style="margin-top:1.4rem">
