           style="width:100%;height:auto;border:1px solid var(--rule)" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption class="dek" style="margin-top:.6rem">Zambrone, Morti 1815, atto 40 — the certified
      extract of 26 May 1849, filed in Briatico, Matrimoni processetti 1849–1854 (Antenati
      an_ua37921205, image 3). The register it was copied from is not among the digitised
      volumes.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <img src={u("/plates/pizzo-colerici1837-atto327.jpg")} alt="Pizzo, morti colerici 1837, atto 327"
           style="width:100%;height:auto;border:1px solid var(--rule)" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption class="dek" style="margin-top:.6rem">Pizzo, registro dei morti colerici 1837, atto
      327 — the certified extract of 20 May 1849, filed in Briatico, Matrimoni processetti 1849–1854
      (Antenati an_ua37921205, image 7).</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">14 September 2026 · Pizzo, the same afternoon</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">And the generation above him, dead on a January night in 1829</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>The marriage act said Pizzo, so Pizzo was opened. The family tree puts
      <strong>Pasquale Prostamo</strong>'s death there on 4 January 1829 and gives no source, which
      meant the answer — if there was one — would be in the first few acts of the year.</p>
      <p>It is act 7. On 14 January 1829, Nicola Nicchella, a porter, and Domenico Belvedere,
      fifty-six, a <em>marinaro</em>, came before the sindaco of Pizzo to say that the evening
      before, at twenty-four o'clock, <strong>Pasquale Prostamo</strong> had died — <em>di anni
      SETTANTACINQUE, VEDOVO DI CATERINA MAJO, nato al Pizzo, di professione MARINARO, domiciliato
      al Pizzo, al Castello</em>.</p>
      <p>Three things follow. The death is on the <strong>thirteenth</strong>, not the fourth: the
      tree is nine days out. He is <strong>seventy-five</strong>, so born about <strong>1753</strong>,
      where the tree says 1760 — the same six-or-seven-year drift this archive has now caught on
      five people. And he was a widower, which names <strong>Caterina Majo</strong>, who was not in
      the tree at all. <em>(This page first said <strong>Muso</strong>. That was a misreading of my own, corrected
      the same day against the chancery's certified copy.)</em></p>
      <p>So the men of this line were sailors for three generations at least: Pasquale, his son
      Antonino, and Antonino's son Domenico, who carried the trade eleven kilometres down the coast
      to Briatico in 1849.</p>
      <p class="dek"><strong>What this does not prove.</strong> The act's two lines for Pasquale's own
      parents are left <strong>blank</strong>, so the generation above him is not reachable from it —
      and since he married before 1809, when civil registration began in Calabria, his marriage is in
      parish books that Antenati does not hold. The tree's first two generations, 1697 and 1726, may
      not be testable online at all. <strong>The descent, though, is no longer in doubt</strong>: later
      the same day a certified copy of this very act turned up in Domenico's marriage bundle, filed as
      the groom's <em>fede di morte dell'avo paterno</em>. See below.</p>
    </div>
    <figure style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <img src={u("/plates/pizzo-morti1829-atto7.jpg")} alt="Pizzo, Morti 1829, atto 7"
           style="width:100%;height:auto;border:1px solid var(--rule)" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption class="dek" style="margin-top:.6rem">Pizzo, Stato civile della restaurazione,
      Morti 1829–1831, atto 7 of 1829 — Archivio di Stato di Catanzaro (Antenati an_ua37926878,
      image 6).</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">14 September 2026</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">The fourth quarter is online, and it is the largest holding this
    archive has</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>This quarter has had <strong>no register work behind it at all</strong>. The Prostamo
      descent is eight generations deep in the family tree and <strong>documented in one of them</strong>
      — Domenico Prostamo, and only because his son's marriage papers named him. Everything above is
      marked <em>untested</em> because nobody had looked.</p>
      <p>There was nothing to stop them. The Archivio di Stato di Catanzaro has
      <strong>five hundred and fifty-five volumes</strong> for Briatico on Antenati, with
      <strong>Potenzoni</strong> — where this family also appears — catalogued separately.</p>

      <div class="rec" style="margin-top:18px">
        <div class="k">From</div><div class="v"><strong>1806</strong>, and civil registration proper
        from <strong>1809</strong> under the French</div><div class="w">before Scilla</div>
        <div class="k">Nati</div><div class="v">Napoleonic 1809–1815, then restaurazione from 1816 —
        <strong>sequential</strong>: <code>ark(Y) = an_ua87718 + (Y − 1816)</code></div>
        <div class="w">86 volumes</div>
        <div class="k">Matrimoni</div><div class="v">1840–1858 — <code>ark(Y) = an_ua87654 + (Y −
        1840)</code> — <strong>then nothing until 1911</strong></div><div class="w">88 volumes</div>
        <div class="k">Morti</div><div class="v">91 volumes, untouched</div><div class="w">—</div>
        <div class="k">Processetti</div><div class="v"><strong>72 volumes</strong> of the papers filed
        with each marriage — including the spouses' own birth certificates</div>
        <div class="w">untouched</div>
      </div>

      <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>The gap explains something.</strong> Alfonso Prostamo
      married Caterina Melluso on <strong>21 May 1887</strong> — squarely inside the 1859–1910 hole —
      which is why this archive has that act from FamilySearch and not from Antenati.</p>
      <p><strong>And generations 4 and 5 are inside the civil era.</strong> Antonino Prostamo, born
      about 1785, and Domenico, about 1820, are both reachable in principle. That is the first time
      anything in this quarter has been reachable at all.</p>

      <h3 style="margin-top:1.8rem;margin-bottom:.6rem">The first read: 1820, and the tree is
      wrong or the year is</h3>
      <p>The tree dates Domenico Prostamo to <strong>1820</strong> and gives no source. That year has
      now been read entire. <strong>Briatico is a small place</strong> — twenty-seven births in the
      whole of 1820 — and its annual Tavola is <strong>alphabetised by the child's given name</strong>,
      the same trap this archive met at Scilla in 1818, where a search under the surname finds nothing
      and proves nothing.</p>
      <p>Read under <strong>D</strong>, the year holds exactly one Domenico: <strong>Insanni
      Domenico</strong>, <em>figlio naturale</em>, born 27 May. <strong>Not a Prostamo.</strong></p>
      <p class="note" style="margin-top:18px">So the tree's 1820 is now a <strong>tested
      negative</strong> rather than an untested assertion — and by this archive's
      <a href={u("/audit")}>own audit rule</a>, a year taken from an undocumented tree date has to be
      widened by at least five years before that negative means anything. 1818, 1819, 1821 and 1822
      are next, and the whole run 1816–1830 costs about one image a year.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="grid g3">
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">{here.length}</div><div class="l">people recorded at Briatico or its hamlets</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">{pl.first || "1697"}</div><div class="l">the earliest birth the archive can name here</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">8</div><div class="l">generations of Prostamo, unbroken</div></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The town</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>Briatico stands on the Tyrrhenian coast about ten kilometres north of Vibo Valentia, on the
      stretch Italians call the <em>Costa degli Dei</em>. The town the family left is not quite the
      town on the map today: old Briatico was wrecked by the <strong>Calabrian earthquake of 1783</strong>
      and again in <strong>1905</strong>, and the modern comune is a rebuilding, a little inland of the
      ruins. Its hamlets — <strong>Potenzoni</strong>, <strong>Mandaradoni</strong>, <strong>San Costantino</strong>
      — appear in this archive as birthplaces in their own right, which is how Calabrian registers
      recorded them.</p>
      <p>That 1783 earthquake matters for a practical reason. It destroyed archives across the
      province, and it is one of the reasons a line that reaches <strong>1697</strong> here is
      unusual enough to be worth checking rather than assuming.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The families</h2>
    <p class="dek" style="margin:.7rem 0 1.2rem">{surnames.length} surnames are recorded at Briatico in
    this archive. Five of them are ancestral to the Brisbane family.</p>
    <p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:14px;line-height:2">
      {surnames.map((s, i) => <>{i > 0 && " · "}<strong>{s}</strong></>)}
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>Everyone the archive places here</h2>
    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th class="m">Dates</th><th>Born</th><th>Died</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {here.sort((a, b) => (a.born || 9999) - (b.born || 9999)).map((p) => (
            <tr>
              <td><a href={u("/people/" + p.slug)}>{p.name}</a></td>
              <td class="m">{[p.born, p.died].filter(Boolean).join("–")}</td>
              <td>{p.birthPlace}</td>
              <td>{p.deathPlace}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">14 September 2026</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Where the Prostamo actually lie, and it is not Nudgee</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>Every Mazza this archive has buried in Australia is at <strong>Nudgee</strong>. Searching
      the Prostamo across Australian cemeteries turns up six, and <strong>not one of them is at
      Nudgee</strong>. They are at Mount Gravatt on the south side of Brisbane, at Nerang on the
      Gold Coast, and — new to this archive entirely — in a <strong>Melbourne</strong> cemetery at
      Glenroy. The two halves of this family came to the same country and did not settle together.</p>
      <p>Three sources agree on one couple. <strong>Maria Assunta</strong> lies in plot 607 at Mount
      Gravatt and <strong>Domenico Francesco</strong> in plot 608. The Commonwealth Gazette
      naturalised him on 1 October 1962 from 38 Park Terrace, Sherwood, and her on 4 July 1966 from
      3 Frank Street, Graceville. The Queensland death index then gives her parents:
      <strong>Gregorio Melluso and Carmela Maria Borello</strong>.</p>
      <p>So she was born a <strong>Melluso</strong> and died a <strong>Prostamo</strong> — the same
      two surnames that married at Briatico on 21 May 1887, when <P n="Alfonso Prostamo" /> married
      <P n="Caterina Melluso" />. Whether the Brisbane couple descend from the Briatico one is
      <strong>untested</strong>, and is recorded here as a hint and not a descent. Her father's own
      death gives two more names, <strong>Gennaro Melluso</strong> and <strong>Maria Limardo</strong>;
      Limardo is a name out of the Potenzoni birth register of 1818, which at least puts that
      household in the right district.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="tw" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th class="m">Dates</th><th>Cemetery</th><th class="m">Plot</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {graves.map((g) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{g.name}</td>
              <td class="m">{[g.born, g.died].filter((x) => x && x !== "\u2014").join(" – ")}</td>
              <td>{g.cemetery}<br /><span class="dek">{g.place}</span></td>
              <td class="m">{g.plot}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:.9rem">Find a Grave, the Queensland registrar's historical index,
    and the Commonwealth of Australia Gazette, read 14 September 2026.</p>
